  "verse": "And know that whatever you do which is unright, it will come back on you; and battle (war) also belongs to unright and great terribleness and you shall avoid it with all good and fair (responsible) means as well as with love… consider, battle (war) is never a way to the creation of peace, freedom, love and consonance (harmony), but only a way of mishappening, hatred, unpeace, unfreedom (bondage), dissonance (disharmony), pillage, revenge and retaliation, robbery, destruction and boundless outrage with bloody deeds (killing) and defilement (violation)…",
  "verseSrc": "Chapter 8, Verse 42",
  "hinge": "§8:42 does not merely condemn war; it enumerates what war *necessarily* produces — *pillage, robbery, destruction* — as though the list were a forecast rather than a complaint. What is happening at Bab al-Mandab is that list arriving: a chokepoint held by an armed movement is not a battle line, it is a toll booth with a missile, and the sequence from *\"maritime embargo\"* to seizure of the strait to strikes on tankers is the mechanism the verse describes. The hinge is therefore **to stop treating the strait as a battlefield objective and treat it as a transit regime** — a legal category that exists and does not require anyone to win. Three documents would do it. **First,** the coalition and the Yemeni government should publish the **withdrawal order** and the current disposition of forces, so that what is held and what is not is a fact rather than a claim. **Second,** the **UN Security Council** should take up Bab al-Mandab as a **navigational-safety matter** — the same instrument used for Hormuz — with a published **convoy and deconfliction schedule** and a named liaison on each side, which converts the Houthis' pledge not to target international shipping from a statement into something that can be tested and, if broken, attributed. **Third,** and this is the one that decides whether Yemen has a government in a year: the internationally recognised authority should state publicly **which territory it still holds and by what force**, because a government that cannot describe its own position cannot negotiate from it. None of the three requires a party to abandon a demand. Each requires a party to write down where it stands — and it is the writing down, not the fighting, that the last six weeks have shown nobody is willing to do."
